\subsection{Executing the search}

We import the [[Search]] class from the core module and execute the query.
The results are then passed to the appropriate formatter.

Since providers automatically paginate to fetch results beyond their per-request
limits, users can request more than a single page of results.

\paragraph{No limit by default.}
\label{sec:no-limit-default}
A search without [[--limit]] fetches every result each provider has,
as [[--limit 0]] does; [[-l N]] takes at most [[N]] per provider
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/301}).  The default used
to be a thousand per provider.  A user who did not know the option
then got a truncated result, which read as capped only where the
provider reports a total (\cref{sec:reported-total} in the providers
chapter), and a record's count was not the database's count but that
of a default nobody had chosen.  What is screened is decided after the
search; the search itself should not lose anything.

The option's default is therefore [[None]], \enquote{not given}, rather
than [[0]]: both search without a limit, but only a search that was
not asked to fetch everything gets the notice below.  Both reach the
providers as [[UNLIMITED_PROVIDER_LIMIT]] (the [[scholar]] module), so
the session records them alike, and a reproduced command says
[[--limit 0]] for both (\cref{sec:search-flags}).
A negative limit is refused as a usage error ([[min=0]]); it used to be
stored and shown as [[--limit -5]].

What \enquote{everything} costs differs per provider; each of these is
in the providers chapter, and [[scholar syntax]] lists them in its
table of searches without a limit (\cref{ch:syntax-cli}):
\begin{description}
\item[Semantic Scholar] An unlimited search goes to the bulk search
  whatever the query ([[s2_uses_bulk]]), so plain words are no longer
  ranked by relevance unless a limit is given, and the bulk search
  stops at its safety cap of a hundred thousand papers
  ([[S2_BULK_SAFETY_CAP]]), recorded as incomplete.
\item[OpenAlex semantic search] It keeps its fifty: the limit is lowered
  to the provider's maximum before the search
  ([[<<refuse what a semantic search cannot do>>]]).
\item[IEEE Xplore] Its limiter allows two hundred requests a day
  ([[LIMITS]]) of at most two hundred records each, so one broad
  unlimited search can spend the day.
\item[OpenAlex] Every page of two hundred works is a request against
  the metered daily budget, and the source-pinned preprint servers
  share that budget.
\end{description}
The other providers page as before, paced by their limiters.

The help says this in one sentence, without the number it used to
carry; the per-provider costs are too many for a help line.

<<constants>>=
SEARCH_LIMIT_HELP = (
    "Maximum results per provider; without it, or with 0, every result "
    "is fetched (see scholar syntax for what that costs per provider)."
)
@

A search that fetches everything should say so when everything is a
lot, while there is still time to stop it and give a limit.  The
providers announce the total they read from the first page
([[first_total_known]], \cref{sec:first-total} in the providers
chapter); during a search without [[--limit]] the command listens, and
for a total of at least [[LARGE_RESULT_NOTICE]] it prints one line on
standard error, so that [[-f json]] and [[-f bibtex]] output stays
pure.  The threshold is our own choice, ten thousand records: a
total that size takes many pages with every provider (the largest page
any of them is asked for holds a thousand), and it is still a tenth of
Semantic Scholar's safety cap.
Nothing is refused or cut: the notice informs, and the user who
typed [[-l 0]] has said already that everything is wanted, so gets no
notice.  The total is the provider's, so Semantic Scholar's estimate
is marked \enquote{about}.  Which providers can announce, and which
cannot, is the providers chapter's business: every provider that
reports a total reads it from the first page, except Semantic
Scholar's relevance search, which an unlimited search never uses.

Three details keep the notice true and single.
\begin{description}
\item[At most once per provider] A provider that restarts its paging
  announces again (arXiv after an empty page, DBLP's zero-hit retry;
  the providers chapter says why it is left so).  The listener
  therefore remembers, in a set held by its closure, which providers it
  has noticed, and a new listener is made for each command, so the
  memory lasts exactly one search (in [[rq]], one run over its
  providers, each searched once).
\item[A provider's own cap] Above Semantic Scholar's safety cap the
  bulk search stops at the cap, so \enquote{fetching all of them} would
  be false.  A provider that has such a cap announces it with the total,
  and the notice then says how many it will fetch and whose cap stops
  it, with the provider's full name ([[PROVIDER_FULL_NAMES]], the review
  module).
\item[One line] The line is printed with [[soft_wrap=True]], as the
  unknown-model error is, so that Rich does not break it at the
  terminal's width (80 columns when standard error is a pipe): a
  script reading standard error gets the notice as one line.
\end{description}

<<constants>>=
LARGE_RESULT_NOTICE = 10_000
<<search command>>=
def large_total_notice(
    name: str, total: int, estimated: bool, cap: int | None = None
) -> str:
    """The notice for a search of `total` results without a limit."""
    about = "about " if estimated else ""
    head = f"{name}: {about}{total:,} results for this query; "
    if cap is not None and total > cap:
        from scholar.review import PROVIDER_FULL_NAMES

        full_name = PROVIDER_FULL_NAMES.get(name, name)
        return head + (
            f"fetching the first {cap:,} ({full_name}'s safety cap; "
            f"give --limit N to take fewer)"
        )
    return head + "fetching all of them (give --limit N to take fewer)"


def large_total_notices():
    """A listener printing the notice at most once per provider."""
    noticed: set[str] = set()

    def notice(name, total, estimated, cap=None):
        if name in noticed or total < LARGE_RESULT_NOTICE:
            return
        noticed.add(name)
        Console(stderr=True).print(
            Text(large_total_notice(name, total, estimated, cap),
                 style="yellow"),
            soft_wrap=True,
        )

    return notice
