Let's verify the default and the notice
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/301}).  The provider is a
small stand-in that announces its total the way every real provider does
on its first page ([[first_total_known]]); the real providers'
announcements are tested against recorded answers in the providers
chapter.  What is tested here is the command's side: which limit reaches
the provider, what the session records and shows, and when the notice is
printed.  The search is recorded in a session so that the record's limit
and the flags [[sessions show]] prints for it can be checked too.

<<test functions>>=
class AnnouncingProvider:
  """A provider whose first page reports `total` matches (no service).

  It announces `times` times, as a provider that restarts its paging
  does, with its own `cap` if it has one.
  """

  MAX_LIMIT = None

  def __init__(self, total, estimated=False, times=1, cap=None):
    self.name = "announcing"
    self.total = total
    self.estimated = estimated
    self.times = times
    self.cap = cap
    self.seen = []
    self.last_error = None
    self.last_retries = 0
    self.last_total = None

  def search(self, query, limit=100, filters=None):
    from scholar.providers import first_total_known

    self.seen.append(limit)
    self.last_total = self.total
    for _ in range(self.times):
      first_total_known(self.name, self.total, self.estimated, self.cap)
    return [Paper(title="Found", authors=["A"], doi="10.1/no-limit")]


