\section{Enriching Papers}

The [[enrich]] command fetches missing abstracts/metadata for papers in a
review session.  This is useful when you've already done a search and want to
fill in abstracts without re-running the search.  It is also where a session
learns which of its papers are open access: the enrichment step consults
Unpaywall (\cref{unpaywall-module}) for every paper with a DOI, and the
command reports the tally.

A session can be large---thousands of papers---while the papers that
need attention now are few: those still pending after a new search,
which are about to be classified and need their abstracts first
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/137}).
Three options and one property of the command serve that case:
\begin{description}
\item[ [[--pending-only]] ] restricts the whole run to the papers still
  pending a decision.
\item[ [[--fields]] ] names what to fill, so a run that only wants
  abstracts can skip the open-access and preprint steps.
\item[ [[--enrich-providers]] ] reorders the providers; with OpenAlex
  first the run profits most from its batched DOI lookups
  (\cref{sec:openalex-doi-batch}).
\item[Saving as it goes] the session is saved after every chunk of
  papers, so a run cut short keeps what it has fetched
  (\cref{sec:enrich-chunks}).
\end{description}

<<enrich command>>=
@app.command()
def enrich(
    session: Annotated[
        str,
        typer.Argument(
            help="Name of the review session to enrich.",
            autocompletion=complete_session_name,
        ),
    ],
    enrich_providers: Annotated[
        Optional[str],
        typer.Option(
            "--enrich-providers",
            help=(
                "Comma-separated provider names to use for enrichment. "
                "Overrides the default order (s2, arxiv, openalex, ieee, "
                "wos, crossref, europepmc, scopus). Putting openalex first "
                "makes the most of its batched DOI lookups. An unknown "
                "name or an empty value is a usage error."
            ),
        ),
    ] = None,
    preprint_search: Annotated[
        bool,
        typer.Option(
            "--preprint-search",
            help=(
                "When no PDF is found, also search preprint servers by "
                "title and authors for a preprint version to adopt. "
                "Authoritative preprint links (OpenAlex, Crossref) are "
                "followed even without this flag."
            ),
        ),
    ] = False,
    pending_only: Annotated[
        bool,
        typer.Option(
            "--pending-only",
            help=(
                "Enrich and check only the papers still pending a "
                "decision; decided papers are left as they are."
            ),
        ),
    ] = False,
    fields: Annotated[
        Optional[str],
        typer.Option(
            "--fields",
            help=(
                "Comma-separated fields to fill (default: "
                "abstract,pdf_url,keywords). pdf_url also turns on the "
                "Unpaywall open-access lookup and the preprint fallback. "
                "Valid: title, authors, abstract, year, venue, pdf_url, "
                "keywords."
            ),
        ),
    ] = None,
) -> None:
    """
    Fetch missing abstracts/metadata for papers in a review session.

    Looks up papers by DOI in available providers to fill in missing
    abstracts/metadata. Providers requiring API keys (IEEE, WoS) are used
    only when configured. Abstracts the other providers lack are asked of
    Crossref and Europe PMC before Scopus. Also looks up open-access
    status and PDF links
    via Unpaywall (requires SCHOLAR_EMAIL) and checks Crossref for
    retractions. When no PDF can be found, a preprint version may be
    adopted in place of the published entry (see --preprint-search).
    The session is saved after every 50 papers, so an interrupted run
    keeps its progress.

    An abstract that looks like something other than an abstract (a
    disclosure statement, a keywords line) does not count as one: the
    other sources are asked, and a real abstract from one of them
    replaces it; otherwise it is kept and listed at the end. Such a
    paper is looked up again on every run, as a paper without an
    abstract is. Abstracts given by hand are never judged.

    Example:
        scholar enrich "machine learning" --pending-only --fields abstract
    """
    <<enrich session papers>>
@

\subsection{Choosing the fields}

The fields are those [[enrich_paper]] can fill
([[ENRICHMENT_FIELD_NAMES]], \cref{enrich-module}); the default is the
set the command always used.
A misspelt name would otherwise be passed on and never filled, and the
run would look like one that found nothing, so we reject it as a usage
error (exit status 2, like a bad [[--proxy]]) naming the valid choices.
The help text lists them too; it cannot read them from the enrichment
module, which the command imports only when it runs.
[[pdf_url]] is more than a field: [[enrich_paper]] runs the Unpaywall
lookup and the preprint fallback only when it is requested, so
[[--fields abstract]] is the cheap run that skips both.
The Crossref currency check is not a field and runs either way.

<<constants>>=
ENRICH_DEFAULT_FIELDS = ["abstract", "pdf_url", "keywords"]
<<enrich command>>=
def parse_enrich_fields(value: str | None) -> list[str]:
    """Return the field names in a `--fields` value, validated.

    Raises `typer.BadParameter` (exit status 2) for an unknown name.
    """
    from scholar.enrich import ENRICHMENT_FIELD_NAMES

    if value is None:
        return list(ENRICH_DEFAULT_FIELDS)
    names = [name.strip() for name in value.split(",") if name.strip()]
    unknown = [name for name in names if name not in ENRICHMENT_FIELD_NAMES]
    if unknown or not names:
        raise typer.BadParameter(
            f"unknown field(s): {', '.join(unknown) or '(none given)'}; "
            f"choose from {', '.join(ENRICHMENT_FIELD_NAMES)}",
            param_hint="'--fields'",
        )
    return names
@

\subsection{Choosing the sources}
\label{sec:enrich-provider-names}

[[--enrich-providers]] names the sources to ask and their order, for
[[scholar enrich]] and for [[scholar search --enrich]] alike.
A name nothing answers to used to be passed on and skipped without a
word, so a typo gave a run that asked fewer sources than the user
thought.
It is now a usage error, like an unknown field, naming the valid
choices: the registered providers with a DOI lookup and the abstract
services Crossref and Europe PMC ([[enrichment_source_names]],
\cref{sec:enrichment-providers}).
Without the option the enrichment's own default order applies, which
[[None]] stands for.
An empty value is an error too, not the default order: it names no
source, and a script that builds the list and ends up with none should
hear so.
[[scholar search]] checks the value as soon as the providers are known,
also without [[--enrich]], where it would otherwise be ignored: a typo
is then reported before the search spends any requests, and the next
run with [[--enrich]] does not meet it.
Both help texts say so.

<<enrich command>>=
def parse_enrich_providers(value: str | None) -> list[str] | None:
    """Return the source names in an `--enrich-providers` value.

    None (the default order) when not given. Raises
    `typer.BadParameter` (exit status 2) for a name no source has.
    """
    if value is None:
        return None
    from scholar.enrich import enrichment_source_names

    valid = enrichment_source_names()
    names = [name.strip() for name in value.split(",") if name.strip()]
    unknown = [name for name in names if name not in valid]
    if unknown or not names:
        raise typer.BadParameter(
            f"unknown source(s): {', '.join(unknown) or '(none given)'}; "
            f"choose from {', '.join(valid)}",
            param_hint="'--enrich-providers'",
        )
    return names
@

