\subsection{The import action}
\label{sec:pdf-import-cli}

[[scholar pdf import FILE (--url URL | --doi DOI)]], with the options
[[--from]] and [[--replace]], hands scholar a PDF that the user fetched by other means,
after a publisher answered the download with a bot-check page, say;
[[import_pdf]] does the work (\cref{sec:pdf-import}).
The command takes its actions as a word and one optional positional
argument, the URL of [[open]] and [[quote]].
The file of [[import]] takes that same place, so the usage of the other
actions stays as it was, and the URL or DOI the file is for comes as an
option, [[--url]] or [[--doi]], exactly one of the two.
[[--from]] records where the file came from; it is optional, since the
user may not know, but the help recommends it, because the record is
the provenance of every passage later quoted from that file.

Refusals exit with status 2, like the cache command's usage errors: a
missing file or key, a file that is not a PDF (with the reason), and a
different PDF already cached for the key without [[--replace]] (with
the hash that is there).
[[clear]] keeps the imported PDFs unless [[--include-imported]], named
like the cache command's [[--include-llm]], and says how many it kept;
[[info]] counts them apart and lists them with [[--list-imported]].
Like the [[quote]] options, these sit on the whole command and are
ignored by the actions they do not concern.

