The DOI the file is stored under is printed apart from the DOI given,
since the two differ whenever the key's normalisation changed something
(case, a resolver prefix, a trailing full stop,
\cref{sec:pdf-import-key}), and that is the DOI a paper must have to
find the file.
A warning (a PDF without text) does not change the exit status: the
file was imported.


The counts come from [[get_pdf_cache_info]]; the row says what a plain
[[clear]] would leave, as the cache command's row for the LLM caches
does.
Orphaned import records (\cref{sec:pdf-cache-imports}) get a row of
their own when there are any: they are the trace of an import that
failed half-way, which the user may want to repeat.
So do temporary files older than an hour, left by a process that died
while writing.
The listing folds long cells instead of cutting them, since a cut
cache file name or hash cannot be matched against anything.

