\section{Session Management}
\label{ch:session-management}

The [[sessions]] command provides access to saved review sessions without
launching the full TUI. Users can:
\begin{description}
\item[sessions list] View all saved review sessions with their statistics
\item[sessions show] Display details of a specific session, including
query-provider pairs when multiple queries were used
\item[sessions export] Generate reports (CSV, \LaTeX{}) from a saved session
\item[sessions resume] Continue reviewing a saved session in the TUI
\item[sessions decide] Record keep/discard decisions non-interactively
\item[sessions check] Hold a semantic search against sessions to find
  works the keyword searches missed
\end{description}

This enables scripted workflows and integration with other tools.

\subsection{The sessions command group}

<<sessions command>>=
sessions_app = typer.Typer(
    name="sessions",
    help="Manage saved review sessions.",
    no_args_is_help=True,
)
app.add_typer(sessions_app, name="sessions")


@sessions_app.command("list")
def sessions_list(
    output_format: Annotated[
        str,
        typer.Option(
            "--format", "-f",
            help="Output format: table or json.",
            autocompletion=complete_display_format,
        ),
    ] = "table",
) -> None:
    """
    List all saved review sessions.

    Shows query, providers, timestamps, and paper counts.
    """
    from scholar.review import list_sessions

    console = Console()
    sessions = list_sessions()

    if not sessions:
        console.print("[dim]No saved sessions found.[/dim]")
        warn_about_unsaved_sessions()
        return

    if output_format == "json":
        print(json.dumps(sessions, indent=2, default=str))
    else:
        table = Table(title="Saved Review Sessions", box=box.HORIZONTALS)
        table.add_column("Name", style="cyan", no_wrap=False)
        table.add_column("Date", style="dim")
        table.add_column("Kept", style="green", justify="right")
        table.add_column("Discarded", style="red", justify="right")
        table.add_column("Pending", style="yellow", justify="right")

        for s in sessions:
            table.add_row(
                Text(session_list_name(s)),
                Text(s["timestamp"].strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M")),
                Text(str(s["kept"])),
                Text(str(s["discarded"])),
                Text(str(s["pending"])),
            )

        console.print(table)
        console.print(f"\n[dim]Total: {len(sessions)} session(s)[/dim]")

    <<warn about sessions without decisions>>
@

A session with search records but no decisions and no research context is
either not screened yet or has lost its screening, as two sessions did in
\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/107}
([[needs_check]], \cref{sec:session-integrity}).  We name such sessions
in a warning after the table rather than in a column: the table and the
JSON on stdout keep their shape for scripts (the JSON gains the
[[needs_check]] field), and the warning goes to stderr, where it reaches
the user in both formats.

The Name column shows what the user types to open a session, and a
name alone is not always that: the named session [[foo]] and the
unnamed session of the query [[foo]] are both called \enquote{foo}, and
a copy holds the name of another file (\cref{sec:cli-session-arguments}).
So when the argument that opens the session differs from its name, the
column adds it in parentheses, [[foo (foo.json)]]; every other row is
unchanged.

<<sessions command>>=
def session_list_name(meta: dict) -> str:
    """The Name cell of `sessions list`: the name, and the argument if not it."""
    name = str(meta["name"])
    argument = meta.get("argument") or name
    return name if argument == name else f"{name} ({argument})"
