Each line under \enquote{Queries by provider:} also says what the search
returned, as recorded in the session (\cref{sec:search-result-error}):
the hit count, or a highlighted \enquote{failed} with the reason.  This
listing is where someone auditing a review reads the query--provider
grid, so it is where a dead provider has to stand out.  Records from
before outcomes were stored show nothing, since nothing is known.
A semantic search's record shows its ranked list as one
(\enquote{top 50, ranked (not a count)}, \cref{sec:ranked-list}) and
without the \enquote{hits:} prefix, which would read as a count.  A merged record from before searches were recorded per provider
reads as one and drops the prefix for the same reason
(\cref{sec:merged-records} in the review module).
The line ends with when the search ran and, for an answer served from
the cache, when that answer was fetched---or that the cache does not
know (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/167}); the
session's own date above is only that of its last change.  The
JSON form marks such a record with [["ranked": true]] (and leaves the
key out otherwise), so a script reading [[hits]] can tell the two
apart as well.
A search that a provider sent to another endpoint than its default
names it after the provider, \enquote{[[s2 (via bulk)]]} or
\enquote{[[s2 (via bulk, most cited first)]]}
(\cref{sec:search-result-via} in the scholar module), and the JSON
form carries [[via]] for it: the bulk search stems its terms and
matches title and abstract only, which a reader comparing the counts
of a row needs to know.

After the outcome comes the order of the answer, when it is recorded
(\cref{sec:search-result-order} in the scholar module):
\enquote{[[order: relevance]]}, \enquote{[[order: citations]]},
\enquote{[[order: date]]} or \enquote{[[order: unknown]]}, and the JSON
form carries [[order]] (the key left out when not recorded).
It is shown for every recorded order, relevance included.  Shown only
when it is not relevance, it would leave a relevance-ordered answer and
a record that does not know its order looking the same, and the
records that do not know are the old ones, among them every Scopus and
Web of Science search made before those two were asked for relevance,
whose answers were newest first
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/303}).  A reader who
has to tell a date-ordered or unordered answer from a ranked one, to
judge what a limit cut off, can do so only if the ranked answers say
so.  A record without an order shows nothing, as before, so the
listing of an older session does not change.

Each line starts with the record's id in brackets
([[[3fa9c1e] s2: plain]]), and the JSON form gives it as [[id]]: seven
hexadecimal digits of a digest of the record's query, provider and
filters ([[record_id]], \cref{sec:retired-searches} in the review
module).  [[sessions retire-search --record ID]]
(\cref{sec:sessions-retire-search}) takes these ids, so that a record
can be picked when its query alone does not tell it apart.  A number
counted down the list would not do: each new run's records are put
first, so after any [[search -n]] the same number names another
record.  The id follows the search instead, also through a re-run.  It
comes first because it names the line, as a list item would; a script
that matched a line by its start (\enquote{[[  s2: plain]]}) must now
allow for it, which is the one change to the listing for a session
without retired searches.
A retired search (\cref{sec:retired-searches} in the review module)
ends with \enquote{(retired 2026-11-20: first version)}, and the
heading then counts both kinds, \enquote{Queries by provider: 5
current, 2 retired}, after the colon, so that the heading stays what
scripts and the tutorials anchor on; the JSON form gives the mark as
[[retired]] and leaves the key out otherwise.  The line is the one
the listing printed before, with the mark added to it: a session of
thousands of records prints no more lines than before
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/273}).

<<sessions command>>=
def _record_provider_label(record) -> str:
    """The provider of a search record, with the endpoint if recorded."""
    via = getattr(record, "via", None)
    return f"{record.provider} (via {via})" if via else record.provider


def _record_outcome_parts(record) -> list:
    """The `sessions show` parts of a search record's outcome.

    `Text.assemble` parts; none when the outcome is unknown.
    """
    from scholar.review import search_outcome_label

    label = search_outcome_label(record)
    if not label:
        return []
    if record.error:
        return [" ", (f"— {label}", "yellow")]
    if is_ranked_provider(record.provider) or record.is_merged:
        return [(f" — {label}", "dim")]
    return [(f" — hits: {label}", "dim")]


def _record_order_parts(record) -> list:
    """The part naming the order of a search's answer, if recorded."""
    order = getattr(record, "order", None)
    return [(f" — order: {order}", "dim")] if order else []


def _record_dates_parts(record) -> list:
    """The parts saying when a search ran and was fetched, if known."""
    from scholar.review import search_dates_label

    label = search_dates_label(record)
    return [(f" — {label}", "dim")] if label else []


def _record_retired_parts(record) -> list:
    """The parts of a retired search's mark, "(retired <date>: note)"."""
    from scholar.review import retired_label

    label = retired_label(record)
    return [" ", (f"({label})", "magenta")] if label else []


def _record_counts_text(records) -> str:
    """" N current, M retired" after the records' heading, or "".

    "" when no record is retired, so the heading reads as before.
    """
    retired = sum(1 for r in records if r.retired is not None)
    if not retired:
        return ""
    return f" {len(records) - retired} current, {retired} retired"


def reset_mark(decision) -> str:
    """The `sessions show` note on a decision's last reset to pending."""
    last = decision.resets[-1]
    return f"reset from {last.get('status')} by {last.get('source')}"


def doubtful_mark(decision, doubt: str) -> str:
    """The `sessions show` note on a doubtful abstract."""
    from scholar.review import fetched_abstract_source

    source = fetched_abstract_source(decision)
    origin = f", from {source}" if source else ""
    return f"doubtful abstract: {doubt}{origin}"


def doubtful_abstracts_line(doubts: dict) -> str:
    """The `sessions show` count of marked abstracts, or "" if none."""
    count = sum(1 for doubt in doubts.values() if doubt)
    if not count:
        return ""
    papers = "paper has" if count == 1 else "papers have"
    return (
        f"Doubtful abstracts: {count} {papers} an abstract that looks "
        "like something other than an abstract (marked below); replace "
        "one with `scholar sessions set-abstract --replace`."
    )


def abstract_data_of(decision, doubt) -> dict:
    """The JSON keys of `sessions show` on a paper's abstract."""
    from scholar.review import fetched_abstract_source

    source = fetched_abstract_source(decision)
    return {
        **({"abstract_doubtful": doubt} if doubt else {}),
        **({"abstract_fetched_from": source} if source else {}),
    }


def abstract_mark(decision) -> str:
    """The `sessions show` note on an abstract given by hand."""
    from scholar.review import hand_abstract_replaced

    origin = decision.abstract_set[-1].get("from")
    since = ", since replaced" if hand_abstract_replaced(decision) else ""
    return f"abstract added by hand, from {origin}{since}"


def relabel_after_reset(decision) -> bool:
    """Tell whether the last relabel describes the current decision.

    True without a reset; never for a reset paper still pending (a
    relabel needs a decision, so it came before the reset); otherwise
    when the last relabel is not older than the last reset.
    """
    if not decision.relabelled:
        return False
    if not decision.resets:
        return True
    if not decision.is_decided:
        return False
    return str(decision.relabelled[-1].get("at") or "") \
        >= str(decision.resets[-1].get("at") or "")


def relabel_mark(decision) -> str:
    """The `sessions show` note on a decision's last source correction."""
    last = decision.relabelled[-1]
    old, new = str(last.get("from")), str(last.get("to"))
    if decision.source.value == new:
        return f"relabelled from {old}"
    return f"relabelled from {old} to {new}, now {decision.source.value}"


def session_paper_line(decision, unmatched_quotes=(), doubt=None) -> Text:
    """Return the text `sessions show` prints for one paper.

    Two lines: the title with provider, tags and marks, then the paper
    id that `sessions decide --paper-id` accepts (`doi:...` or
    `hash:...`); a third with the agent's reason when there is one.
    A mark follows the title when `unmatched_quotes` holds a quote of
    its reasons that the record does not, and when `doubt` gives why
    its abstract looks like something other than an abstract. Stored
    text is appended as plain text, never read as markup, so it prints
    as stored.
    """
    paper = decision.paper
    line = Text("  • ")
    line.append(paper.title or "")
    for note, style in paper_line_notes(decision, unmatched_quotes, doubt):
        line.append(" ")
        line.append(note, style)
    line.append("\n    ")
    line.append(paper.id, "dim")
    reason = agent_reason_line(decision)
    if reason:
        line.append("\n    ")
        line.append(reason, "dim")
    return line


def paper_line_notes(decision, unmatched_quotes=(), doubt=None) -> list:
    """The `(text, style)` notes after a paper's title in `sessions show`.

    The provider and the tags (dimmed), then the marks: who decided if
    no human did (magenta), the last relabel, reset and abstract given
    by hand, an earlier classifier trace (dimmed), a quote of its
    reasons the record does not hold and a doubtful abstract (yellow).
    """
    from scholar.review import unreviewed_mark

    notes = []
    if decision.provider:
        notes.append((f"({decision.provider})", "dim"))
    if decision.tags:
        notes.append((f"[{', '.join(decision.tags)}]", "dim"))
    mark = unreviewed_mark(decision)
    if mark:
        notes.append((f"({mark})", "magenta"))
    if relabel_after_reset(decision):
        notes.append((f"({relabel_mark(decision)})", "dim"))
    if decision.resets:
        notes.append((f"({reset_mark(decision)})", "dim"))
    if decision.abstract_set:
        notes.append((f"({abstract_mark(decision)})", "dim"))
    if earlier_classifier_trace(decision):
        notes.append((f"(llm trace: {EARLIER_TRACE_LABEL})", "dim"))
    if unmatched_quotes:
        notes.append((f"({QUOTE_MARK})", "yellow"))
    if doubt:
        notes.append((f"({doubtful_mark(decision, doubt)})", "yellow"))
    return notes
@

\paragraph{Printing a long listing.}
\label{sec:sessions-show-speed}
A session of 10\,000 decisions has 10\,000 paper lines, and printing
them one [[Console.print]] each made [[sessions show]] take most of a
minute (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/273}).  Rich
does three things per call given a string: it parses the markup
(replacing emoji codes on the way), runs its default highlighter (a
handful of regular expressions that colour numbers, URLs and the like)
over the text, and wraps and renders the result.  A profile of the
old code on a synthetic session of 10\,000 decisions (20\,MB) put the
highlighter at a fifth of the command and the per-call rendering at
most of the rest.

Printing only the three paper lists of that session to a file, in one
process on a loaded machine (load average 10.6 on 8 cores), took
23.4\,s one call per line as before; 13.9\,s one call per line without
the highlighter; 17.6\,s in one call per group with it; 7.9\,s in one
call per group without it; 7.5\,s in calls of 200 lines without it.
Writing the plain text of Rich's wrapped lines ourselves, past Rich's
rendering, took 7.6\,s: what is left is parsing the markup and
wrapping, so that path would only add a second way of printing to
keep in step with Rich.

The markup was also wrong.  The session's text went into it escaped,
but escaping is not enough: the tag list's own closing bracket can
pair with a bracket inside a tag.  The tags \texttt{x[bold},
\texttt{plain} and \texttt{a]b[c} printed as \texttt{[xb}, the rest
taken for the tags \texttt{[bold, plain, a]} and \texttt{[c]}, which is
Rich's conceal style; once a group was printed in one call, that style
ran over every later paper of the group, hiding it on a terminal.  Rich's
[[escape]] doubles a backslash at the end of a text, which only
collapses before a bracket, so a title, tag or provider ending in a
backslash printed two; and every stored text went through Rich's emoji
replacement, so a title with [[a:b:c]] printed [[a]], an emoji and
[[c]].  Stored text must print as stored.  So [[session_paper_line]]
builds a [[Text]] instead of markup: our own pieces are appended with
their styles, the stored strings as plain text, which Rich never parses
as markup, never highlights (the highlighter runs only on strings
given to [[print]]) and never searches for emoji codes.  Printing the
paper lists by this route was at least as fast as joined markup: three
runs of each, alternately, at load averages of 7 to 9, took 6.9, 4.7
and 4.9\,s of CPU time against 7.4, 6.9 and 7.7\,s.

The texts are printed a few hundred papers per call, not a whole group
at once.  One call for a group holds the whole group's rendering in
memory and shows a terminal nothing until all of it is done.  Measured
on the same session (paper lists only, load average 7.4): one call per
group took 5.1\,s, calls of 1000, 500, 200, 100, 50, 20 and 1 papers
took 4.5, 3.7, 3.6, 3.7, 4.4, 5.5 and 8.0\,s; Python's allocation peak
([[tracemalloc]]) was 41\,MB for one call per group against 7\,MB for
calls of 200 (the output itself is 2.3\,MB).  Two hundred is in the
flat part of both curves.
The whole command, piped to a file, run three times alternately with
the code from before this change (one call per paper line) at load
averages of 5 to 9, took 6.3, 6.1 and 7.5\,s (wall clock; 6.0 to
7.3\,s of CPU time) against 25.0, 22.9 and 23.7\,s (22.5 to
24.4\,s), with the same output bytes and the same peak memory of the
process, 97\,MB.  Of the rest, loading the session takes about 1.3\,s
and the copy analysis about 0.9\,s.

Apart from what was wrong, nothing changes in what a pipe receives.
Rich wraps a text with several lines one line at a time
([[Text.wrap]] splits at the newlines before it wraps), so one call
wraps exactly as one call per line did, and a console without a colour
system (output to a pipe or a file) writes no styles.  The listing
pinned below holds this to the byte, and compares the paper lines'
styles on a terminal too: they are the ones the markup gave, without
the highlighter's colouring of numbers and URLs inside titles, ids and
reasons.  The other lines keep the highlighter (below).

<<sessions command>>=
PAPER_LINES_PER_PRINT = 200


def print_paper_lines(
    con: Console, decisions, unmatched: dict, doubts: dict | None = None,
) -> None:
    """Print the `session_paper_line` of each decision, a few hundred
    papers per call.

    `unmatched` maps `id(decision)` to the quotes of its reasons that
    its record does not hold, `doubts` to why its abstract is doubtful.
    """
    doubts = doubts or {}
    for start in range(0, len(decisions), PAPER_LINES_PER_PRINT):
        chunk = decisions[start:start + PAPER_LINES_PER_PRINT]
        con.print(Text("\n").join(
            session_paper_line(d, unmatched[id(d)], doubts.get(id(d)))
            for d in chunk
        ))
@

\paragraph{The other lines.}
The lines above and below the paper lists print stored text too: the
session's query and name, the searches' queries, providers, filters,
errors and retirement notes, the sources' labels (an unknown source is
shown by its stored value), the screeners' names in the double-screen
lines, the themes and the copies' titles, tags and venues.  They were
markup strings with the stored parts escaped, and had the same faults
as the paper lines: a stored string ending in a backslash printed two.
So they are [[Text]]s as well, assembled by [[print_show_line]] from
plain strings and [[(text, style)]] pairs, the form
[[Text.assemble]] takes, with the stored strings as plain text.  They
are few, so they keep Rich's highlighter, applied as Rich applies it
to a printed string ([[Console.render_str]]): it highlights the plain
text and then copies the line's own styles over it, so where the two
meet, the line's style wins, and on a terminal every colour stays
where the markup put it.

<<sessions command>>=
def print_show_line(con: Console, *parts) -> None:
    """Print one `sessions show` line made of `parts`, as plain text.

    `parts` are strings or `(text, style)` pairs, as `Text.assemble`
    takes them; no part is read as markup. The console's highlighter
    is applied as `Console.print` applies it to a string.
    """
    text = Text.assemble(*parts)
    highlighted = con.highlighter(text.plain)
    highlighted.copy_styles(text)
    con.print(highlighted)
@

\paragraph{The listing pinned.}
\label{sec:sessions-show-pinned}
The listing is read by people and, piped, by scripts and agents that
[[grep]] it, so a change that only makes it faster must not change a
byte of it (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/273}).
Let's pin it on a session that has every kind of line [[sessions
show]] prints: search records of every outcome (hits, a total and
a cap, a failure, a ranked list, a merged record, filters, dates,
an endpoint, an old record without an outcome, a retired search;
each with its id in brackets, \cref{sec:retired-searches} in the
review module), the summary lines (who decided, the quote check, the
decisions made without an abstract, screenings and the double screen),
themes, the three groups with
every mark (unreviewed, relabelled, relabelled and changed since,
reset, abstract added by hand, an earlier classifier trace, a quote
not found), a reason cut at [[SHOW_REASON_CHARS]], a paper without a
provider and one whose provider holds brackets, and the four kinds
of copies.  Each kind of stored string meets Rich somewhere, so each
holds something Rich could read as its own.  The titles: one long
enough to wrap, with numbers and a URL in it; one with Rich markup and
a backslash; one with accented and Chinese letters (two cells wide
each, which moves the place where a line wraps) and an emoji code; one
with emoji codes inside words; one ending in a backslash, with a
provider and a tag that end in one too; and tags that hold brackets.
The themes: one ends in a backslash, one holds a word in brackets,
one is an emoji code.  The session's name holds brackets and its query
an emoji code and a final backslash.  A search's query holds an emoji code
inside a word, another search's provider, query and error end in a
backslash, and the retired search's note ends in one.  The screener
beside the classifier is named with an emoji code.  The session holds
a research context with markup, an emoji code and a backslash, and an
LLM sample, of which [[show]] prints nothing.  A second, small session
without searches lists its providers in their place, one of them with
brackets.

The test runs the command as a pipe sees it, at 80 and at 200 columns
([[COLUMNS]]; the colour variables are unset, so Rich writes no
styles), and compares with the text recorded from the code before the
change, one call per line, written out as escaped Python strings so
that every trailing space and every non-ASCII character is visible in
the source.  Only the lines that code printed wrong were recorded from
the new code, each now as stored: \texttt{(open[alex])} where the old
code printed \texttt{(open)}; the whole tag list where it printed
\texttt{[xb}; \texttt{:smile:}, \texttt{key:id:value} and the
screener's name, the session's query and the emoji theme (in the
Themes line and the tag list) where it printed emoji (which moves
where two lines wrap at 80 columns); and one backslash where it printed two (the
theme, in the Themes line and the paper's tag list, the title, the
tags, the search's provider, which made its line wrap at 80 columns,
and the retirement note); and, in the small session, the provider
with brackets, which the old code put into the markup unescaped.  A
backslash at the end of a stored string printed right in the old code
where a closing tag followed it or more text was escaped with it, and
an escaped word in brackets printed right too: the search's query and
error and the bracketed theme are recorded from the old code.  The line
on decisions made without an abstract
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/293}) came later and
was added where it appears, after the quote check; nothing else
changed.
The order of an answer (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/303})
came later too: one Scopus search records [[order: relevance]], whose
line wraps before the order's value at 80 columns, and the heading
counts it.  Since \url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/301} a
command always carries its limit (\cref{sec:search-flags}), so the
search of [[key:id:value]] has the limit of the old default, a
thousand, which the code before that printed no flag for: its line is
the one that shows the change, [[--limit 1000]].

