\paragraph{Writing the export all or nothing.}
\label{sec:sessions-export-atomic}
An export of several files is only useful as a set: the [[.tex]] cites
keys from the [[.bib]], and a [[.csv]] next to them is expected to list the
same decisions.  [[sessions export -f all]] used to write the [[.csv]]
first and the [[.bib]] and [[.tex]] afterwards, so when the \LaTeX{} step
crashed (once on a list-valued venue) the user was left with a fresh
[[.csv]] beside a stale [[.tex]] that still compiled, and a traceback that
had scrolled past (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/83}).
A partial export is worse than none.

So the generators now write into a staging directory next to the targets
([[staged_outputs]], \cref{sec:atomic-write}), and the files are moved
into place only when every format has been built.  The generators need no
change: each receives a path in the staging directory under the final
file name, and the \LaTeX{} report derives its [[.bib]] from that path as
before.  Any failure, a bug in a generator as much as a missing output
directory, ends in an error line and exit code~1.  The traceback goes
to the debug log, where [[--verbose]] shows it.

The message says what the failure left, because a user who reads that
nothing was written will trust the old files beside it.  A failure while
the files are produced leaves the earlier outputs untouched, since
nothing has been moved yet.  A failure while they are moved into place
is undone by [[staged_outputs]] (\cref{sec:staged-outputs}), which
first checks every target, so that a directory where a file should go
stops the export before anything moves, and otherwise puts back what it
had replaced.  In both cases the earlier outputs are as they were, and
the message says so, adding that the export was rolled back when files
had been moved.  Only when putting a file back fails too are the
outputs mixed, and then the message lists, per file, whether it holds
the new version, the earlier one or none, and where an earlier version
is kept (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/320}: the message
used to claim that nothing was written in this case as well).

