    Optional[bool],
    typer.Option(
        "--spreadsheet-safe/--no-spreadsheet-safe",
        help=f"CSV (-f csv, -f all): {SPREADSHEET_SAFE_HELP}",
    ),
] = None,
<<refuse [[--spreadsheet-safe]] without the CSV>>=
if spreadsheet_safe is not None and format_type not in ("csv", "all"):
    Console(stderr=True).print(
        "[red]--spreadsheet-safe applies to the CSV only "
        "(-f csv or -f all).[/red]"
    )
    raise typer.Exit(2)
csv_spreadsheet_safe = (
    SPREADSHEET_SAFE_DEFAULT if spreadsheet_safe is None
    else spreadsheet_safe
)
@

Let's verify both commands end to end, with invented data.  The export
gets the prefix with [[-f csv]] and with [[-f all]] and writes the
stored text without the flag; with [[-f latex]] the flag is refused and
nothing is written.  The search's data row gets the prefix in its text
cells and not in the year, while its [[#]] lines stay as they are, and
its default output is unchanged; the flag with [[-f json]] is refused
before the provider is asked.  The search's tests sit here, not with
its formatter, because the formatter's tests are methods of one shared
class.

