\paragraph{A blind screen sheet.}
\label{sec:screen-sheet}
An agent that screens the records a second time with [[sessions
screen]] (\cref{sec:sessions-screen}) is blind only if it never reads
the first verdict, and a session file holds every verdict.  So
[[sessions export -f screen-sheet]] writes what a screener needs and no
more: one line per paper, its id, title, year, venue and abstract,
separated by tabs, under a header line naming the five columns.  No
status, tag, source, confidence, reason or other screening is on it.
The id is the one [[sessions screen --verdicts-file]] takes, so the
verdicts can be written beside the sheet's lines.  Scholar cannot know
whether the screener read only the sheet; that is why [[sessions screen
--blind]] records blindness as declared (\cref{sec:agreement-reports}
in the agreement module).

The papers are those a second screen is for, since its verdicts are
compared with a first one and a pending paper has none: the decided
ones, as
[[llm classify --second-screen --over all]] selects them, or with
[[--sample ID]] those of a review sample, without the ones reset to
pending since the draw ([[second_screen_candidates]],
\cref{sec:second-screen} in the LLM review module).  Copies of one work
under one id are one line, as [[sessions screen]] takes a verdict per
id.  [[--undecided-by NAME]] leaves out the papers on which a screener
of that name already has a verdict: a counted screening of that name,
or the decision itself when that screener made or confirmed it, which
[[sessions screen]] would refuse to screen again.  A name is matched
as written, whatever the kind, since the sheet does not know which
kind of screener will read it; a paper stays on the sheet while any of
its copies lacks the name's verdict.

A tab inside a field would start a new column and a line break a new
line, so both become spaces: every character Python's
[[str.splitlines]] breaks at, and the tab.  The other characters stay
as stored.

The header and the rows are written by the standard library's [[csv]]
module, tab-separated, with [[QUOTE_NONE]] and no quote character, so
that a quote mark in a title is written as it is stored, as the
unquoted format of the reasons and verdicts files has it
(\cref{sec:sessions-decide}), and a newline ends each line.  Such a
writer refuses only a field holding a tab or a line end, which
[[sheet_field]] has already turned into spaces (checked on Python
3.12, 3.13 and 3.14: the bytes equal a tab-join of the fields).  The
comment lines a sheet in batches begins with
(\cref{sec:sheet-batches}) are no CSV rows and are written first, as
text.  Reading is another matter: the reasons and verdicts files keep
their own reader (\cref{sec:batch-against-sheet}), since [[csv.reader]]
ends a line at a lone carriage return, which that format keeps inside
a field.

<<sessions command>>=
SCREEN_SHEET_COLUMNS = ("id", "title", "year", "venue", "abstract")
SHEET_SEPARATORS = re.compile("[\t\n\r\v\f\x1c\x1d\x1e\x85\u2028\u2029]")


def sheet_field(value) -> str:
    """`value` as one field of a screen sheet: no tab, no line break."""
    return SHEET_SEPARATORS.sub(" ", "" if value is None else str(value))


def has_verdict_by(decision, name: str) -> bool:
    """Whether a screener called `name` has a verdict on `decision`."""
    from scholar.review import counted_screenings, verdict_screener

    return verdict_screener(decision)["name"] == name or any(
        record.get("name") == name
        for record in counted_screenings(decision)
    )


def screen_sheet_decisions(
    session, sample: str | None = None, undecided_by: str | None = None
) -> list:
    """The decisions a screen sheet lists, one per paper id."""
    from scholar.llm_review import second_screen_candidates

    selection = second_screen_candidates(
        session, over=None if sample else "all", sample=sample
    )
    chosen: dict[str, object] = {}
    for decision in selection.decisions:
        if undecided_by is not None and has_verdict_by(
            decision, undecided_by
        ):
            continue
        chosen.setdefault(decision.paper.id, decision)
    return list(chosen.values())


def screen_sheet_rows(decisions) -> list[list[str]]:
    """The lines of a screen sheet: id, title, year, venue, abstract."""
    return [
        [sheet_field(value) for value in (
            d.paper.id, d.paper.title, d.paper.year, d.paper.venue,
            d.paper.abstract,
        )]
        for d in decisions
    ]


def write_screen_sheet(
    path: Path, rows: list[list[str]], comments=()
) -> None:
    """Write the header and `rows` as tab-separated UTF-8 lines.

    Each `(key, value)` of `comments` comes first as `# key: value`.
    """
    import csv

    with path.open("w", newline="", encoding="utf-8") as sheet:
        sheet.write("".join(f"# {key}: {sheet_field(value)}\n"
                            for key, value in comments))
        writer = csv.writer(sheet, delimiter="\t", quoting=csv.QUOTE_NONE,
                            quotechar=None, lineterminator="\n")
        writer.writerow(SCREEN_SHEET_COLUMNS)
        writer.writerows(rows)
@

