def pending_sheet_decisions(session) -> list:
    """The pending decisions a first-screen sheet lists, one per paper id."""
    from scholar.review import pending_papers

    return pending_papers(session)
@

\paragraph{A first tier.}
\label{sec:sheet-tier}
A screen of every returned record is often more than a review can do
with the same care, and the rule of
\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/302} screens a first
tier and counts the rest: the head of each relevance-ranked answer and
what several databases returned (\cref{sec:screening-tier} in the
review module).  [[--top K]], [[--min-providers N]]
and [[--provider P]]
(repeatable, narrowed by [[--query-like TEXT]]) select that tier
from the papers the sheet would list, pending or decided: a paper stays
on the sheet when it meets at least one of the given conditions
([[screening_tier]]).  Without them the sheet is the one it always was,
byte for byte.

A tier is only as good as the statement of what it left out, so the
counts are printed ([[tier_lines]]) and also written as JSON beside the
sheet, [[BASE-tier.json]], with the session file and the selection, so
that a method can quote \enquote{screened [[N]] of [[M]]} from a file
rather than from a terminal.  It is written for a single sheet as for
sheets in batches: the counts concern the selection, not how it is cut.
The manifest of batches stays a plain table without comment lines
(\cref{sec:sheet-batches}), so the counts do not go there.

The options make sense only for the screen sheet and are refused with
another format, as its other options are.  A place or a number of
providers below one would select everything or nothing and is refused;
[[--query-like]] narrows [[--provider]] and is refused alone.  A
provider that no current search of the session names is refused once
the session is read, naming the ones it has: a mistyped name would
otherwise select nothing, silently.

