A sheet exported without the tier options lists every paper, so the
counts of an earlier tiered export of the same base name,
[[BASE-tier.json]], no longer describe the sheet beside them, and a
reader who found both would quote the wrong selection.  Such a file is
removed, and the export says so.  The removal is part of the export's
last step: [[staged_outputs]] takes the names to remove
([[stale_tiers]]) and removes them only when every staged file has been
moved into place, undoing the removal with the moves when one fails
(\cref{sec:staged-outputs}), so a failed export never removes the
counts while leaving the sheet they describe.  The decision is made
before the staging, from the file as it is then; that is also where the
sheet's own path is settled, since the stale file's name derives from
it.  Batches left from an earlier, longer export are not removed: that
was so before tiers and is not about them.

The name is ours, but a file of that name need not be: a user may have
put notes there, or a file of another tool.  So it is removed only when
it reads as tier counts scholar wrote, a JSON object holding every key
of [[TIER_FILE_KEYS]], the keys [[<<keep the screen sheet's tier>>]]
writes at its top level besides the counts.  Any other file is left
where it is, with one warning line naming it, since the sheet beside it
does not follow it either.

<<sessions command>>=
TIER_FILE_KEYS = ("session", "selection", "rule", "selected",
                  "not_selected")


def is_tier_file(path: Path) -> bool:
    """Whether `path` holds tier counts as the screen sheet writes them."""
    try:
        data = json.loads(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError, json.JSONDecodeError):
        return False
    return isinstance(data, dict) and all(k in data for k in TIER_FILE_KEYS)
