def test_a_failed_restore(self, tmp_path, monkeypatch):
  import os
  from scholar.utils import KEPT_PREFIX, STAGING_PREFIX

  sheets = self._earlier_export(tmp_path, monkeypatch)
  real_replace = os.replace

  def failing_move(source, target, *args, **kwargs):
    if (STAGING_PREFIX in str(source) and Path(target).name == "rest.tsv"
        or KEPT_PREFIX in str(source)
        and Path(target).name == "rest-tier.json"):
      raise OSError(28, "No space left on device")
    return real_replace(source, target, *args, **kwargs)

  monkeypatch.setattr(os, "replace", failing_move)
  result = sheets._export(tmp_path, "--rest-sample", "3", "--seed", "320")
  assert result.exit_code == 1
  text = " ".join(result.output.split())
  assert ("Export failed after the draw was recorded and could not be "
          "rolled back") in text
  assert f"Holding the new version: {tmp_path / 'rest-tier.json'}" in text
  assert f"Holding the earlier version: {tmp_path / 'rest.tsv'}" in text
  assert "the screen sheet may be among the files listed above" in text
  assert "was not written" not in text
@

The dry run draws before the staging, prints what a real run would
print and returns; the session is not saved, so the draw is forgotten.
It prints no id: a real run draws the same papers but records them
under an id of its own (\cref{sec:tier-sample} in the LLM review module
says why an id ends in random digits), and an id printed here would
name no draw.
A real run with the same seed draws the same papers, as long as the
pending papers and the tier are the same.

