\paragraph{Sheets in batches.}
\label{sec:sheet-batches}
Screeners given several hundred records at once have filled the rest
of a batch with one verdict, one reason and one confidence
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/291}), and the issue
that asked for batches (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/292})
reports that it happened in batches of about 365 records.  A batch
check can find such a batch afterwards (\cref{sec:decide-batch-check}),
but only when the rows say which batch they came from, and only a
batch of known extent can be redone as a unit.  So [[--batch-size N]]
writes the sheet as numbered sheets of at most [[N]] records,
[[BASE-001.tsv]], [[BASE-002.tsv]], \dots, in the order of the single
sheet, and a manifest [[BASE-manifest.tsv]] that lists them: batch id,
file name, number of records, first and last id.  Without the option
the export writes the one sheet it always wrote, byte for byte.

Scholar imposes no batch size.  The screening protocol this was written
for recommends batches of about 100 records, and the help says why that
is only a recommendation: in the runs behind it, defaulted rows were
frequent in batches of several hundred, rare at 100 or fewer, and in
one run still came back in batches of 100.  What the option does is
give each batch an id, which lets a bad batch be found and redone.
Nothing more is known about what a batch size achieves, so nothing more
is claimed, and a default would claim it.

Each sheet starts with three comment lines, [[# batch: ID]],
[[# session: FILE]] and [[# records: N]], before the header and the
rows of the single sheet.  The reasons and verdicts files skip comment
lines and read [[# batch:]] as the batch of the lines after it
(\cref{sec:batch-ids-in-files}), so a screener that writes its verdicts
below the sheet's comment lines returns its batch id with them.  The
session line names the session file (not its path, which is the
exporter's own) so that a sheet found later can be traced back.

The batch id is the selection's digest and the sheet's number,
[[5be0c1d2-001]]: readable enough to type, and the same for the same
session file, selection and batch size, so that exporting again gives
the sheets the same ids, while another selection (a screener's verdicts
recorded since, with [[--undecided-by]]) or another size gives new
ones and cannot be confused with the old sheets.  The digest is the
first [[SHEET_DIGEST_CHARS]] hexadecimal digits of the SHA-256 of the
session file's name, the batch size and the sheet's ids in order.  A
first-screen sheet and a second-screen sheet of the same session and
size therefore get other ids, because a paper is either pending or
decided: their id lists can only be equal if every paper id held both
a pending and a decided copy and no other paper existed, and the two
sheets would then list the same records anyway.  It
depends only on what selects the rows, not on the records' titles or
abstracts, which [[scholar enrich]] may fill in between two exports of
the same sheet.

