@

Only a newline ends a line, and a carriage return before it is dropped
(a file saved on Windows).  [[str.splitlines]] would also split at a
lone carriage return, a line separator (U+2028), a next-line
character, a form feed and other
characters that a reason copied from a PDF may hold: the reason would
be cut in two, the second half read as a line without a tab, and every
error after it would name a line number one higher than the editor
shows.  Splitting at newlines alone keeps such characters inside the
reason, where they are stored as they were written.

A line holds the id, the required columns and, after them, the
optional ones.  An empty optional column (a spreadsheet's export of an
empty cell) counts as absent rather than as a bad value.  A column more
than the layout has comes from a tab inside the reason or from tabs
after the last column; the message names each extra column and what it
holds, since an empty one (two trailing tabs) is otherwise invisible.
The file is not quoted, as a CSV file would be: quote marks are part
of the reason and stored as written.  The problems of one line are
joined in the order of its columns, the empty id first.

<<parse one line of a batch file>>=
columns = raw.split("\t")
required = 1 + sum(not column.optional for column in layout.columns)
if len(columns) < required:
    problems.append(f"line {number}: {layout.too_few}")
    continue
if len(columns) > 1 + len(layout.columns):
    first_extra = 2 + len(layout.columns)
    extra = ", ".join(
        f"{index} ({column!r})"
        for index, column in enumerate(
            columns[first_extra - 1:], start=first_extra
        )
    )
    problems.append(
        f"line {number}: extra column {extra} after {layout.last} "
        f"({layout.holds}; a tab inside a reason starts a new column)"
    )
    continue
paper = columns[0].strip()
line_problems = [] if paper else ["empty id"]
values = []
for index, column in enumerate(layout.columns, start=1):
    text_value = columns[index] if index < len(columns) else None
    if text_value is None or (column.optional and not text_value.strip()):
        values.append(None)
        continue
    value, problem = column.read(text_value)
    if problem:
        line_problems.append(problem)
    values.append(value)
if not line_problems and layout.check is not None:
    line_problems.extend(layout.check(tuple(values)))
if line_problems:
    problems.append(f"line {number}: {'; '.join(line_problems)}")
    continue
entries.append((number, paper, tuple(values), batch))
@

\paragraph{Batch ids in a file.}
\label{sec:batch-ids-in-files}
A screener is given its records in batches, and a batch is where
defaulted rows happen: a screener that reads some records of a long
batch and fills the rest with one reason
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/292}, and the batch
check of \cref{sec:decide-batch-check}).  So a file may say which batch
its lines came from, in a comment line [[# batch: ID]], which the screen
sheets of [[sessions export -f screen-sheet --batch-size]] begin with
(\cref{sec:sheet-batches}).  It is a comment line because the lines of
a file are its verdicts and every comment line was skipped already: a
file written for an older Scholar, or one that copies the sheet's
comment lines, means the same as before.  A later [[# batch:]] line
starts a new batch for the lines after it, so that the files of several
batches can be concatenated into one; lines before the first have no
batch.  The id is stored with each verdict's trace
([[agent_batch]] of a decision, [[batch]] of a screening, in the review
module).

The id is free text and is not looked up anywhere: the sheet may have
been made by another tool, on another machine, and Scholar keeps no
list of the sheets it wrote.  It is read as [[read_batch_id]] reads it,
white space collapsed (a line break pasted into it is no part of the
name), and refused when it is empty or longer than
[[MAX_BATCH_ID_CHARS]] (100 characters, far more than Scholar's own ids
of a dozen, but bounded since every record of the batch stores it).  A
refused id is a bad line like any other, named and refused before the
session is loaded; the lines after it are then read without a batch,
which does not matter since nothing is saved.  The word [[batch]] is
matched in any case and the colon may have spaces before it, but
[[# batch size: 100]] or [[# batches]] name no batch.

<<note the batch a comment line names>>=
marked = BATCH_LINE.fullmatch(raw.strip())
if marked:
    batch, problem = read_batch_id(marked.group(1))
    if problem:
        problems.append(f"line {number}: # batch: {problem}")
        batch = None
<<sessions command>>=
BATCH_LINE = re.compile(r"#\s*batch\s*:(.*)", re.IGNORECASE)
MAX_BATCH_ID_CHARS = 100


def read_batch_id(text: str) -> tuple[str, str | None]:
    """A batch id with white space collapsed, and its problem, if any."""
    batch = " ".join(text.split())
    if not batch:
        return batch, "empty batch id"
    if len(batch) > MAX_BATCH_ID_CHARS:
        return batch, (
            f"batch id of {len(batch)} characters, over the limit of "
            f"{MAX_BATCH_ID_CHARS}"
        )
    return batch, None
@

[[--batch ID]] gives one batch id to a run whose file names none, or to
the papers of a [[--reason]] or of a single verdict.  It is refused with
a file that names its batches: two sources of one id would have to be
ranked, and a caller who passes [[--batch]] with a file of
concatenated batches most likely means something the file already says
otherwise.

<<sessions command>>=
def lines_with_batch(lines: list, batch: str | None, path) -> tuple:
    """`lines` with `batch` (from `--batch`) given to each, and problems.

    A file that names a batch on any line keeps its own ids and makes
    `--batch` a problem.
    """
    if batch is None:
        return lines, []
    named = next((entry for entry in lines if entry.batch is not None),
                 None)
    if named is not None:
        return lines, [
            f"{path}: names its batches (# batch: {named.batch}, before "
            f"line {named.line}); --batch is for a file without "
            "# batch: lines"
        ]
    return [dataclasses.replace(entry, batch=batch) for entry in lines], []
