The hazard is not particular to this chunk.  A command function here
is assembled from chunks written far apart, so a chunk's author does
not see the parameter list it is tangled under.  A test therefore reads
the tangled module and fails when any of its functions binds one of its
own parameters by a statement or expression whose binding looks local
to a block but is not:
\begin{itemize}
\item a [[for]] target, a [[with ... as]] target or an
  [[except ... as]] name;
\item a walrus target ([[name := ...]]), also inside a comprehension,
  where it binds in the enclosing function;
\item a [[match]] capture: [[case name:]], [[case ... as name]],
  [[case [*name] ]] and [[case {**name}]];
\item an [[import]] inside the function, which binds its [[as]] name
  or, without one, the first part of the module's name.
\end{itemize}
Only names in store context count: [[for name.x in y]] and
[[for name[i] in y]] assign to an attribute or an item of the
parameter's value and leave the parameter itself bound, so the test
accepts them.  An ordinary assignment to a parameter is not flagged
either: the commands normalise their options that way on purpose
([[by = by.strip()]], [[source = "human"]] when stdin is a terminal),
and such a line says plainly that it replaces the value.

The walk stops at nested functions, lambdas and classes, whose
bindings are their own; a nested function's parameters are checked as
those of a function of its own.  It does enter comprehensions, because
a walrus there binds in the function.  A comprehension's own [[for]]
clauses are [[ast.comprehension]] nodes, not [[for]] statements, so
they are not counted: they bind in the comprehension's own scope and
cannot reach a parameter.

<<test functions>>=
def _parameter_rebindings(source):
  """Where a function of `source` rebinds one of its parameters."""
  import ast

  scopes = (ast.FunctionDef, ast.AsyncFunctionDef, ast.Lambda, ast.ClassDef)

  def own_nodes(function):
    stack = list(function.body)
    while stack:
      node = stack.pop()
      yield node
      if not isinstance(node, scopes):
        stack.extend(ast.iter_child_nodes(node))

  def stored(targets):
    return [name.id for target in targets for name in ast.walk(target)
            if isinstance(name, ast.Name)
            and isinstance(name.ctx, ast.Store)]

  def bindings(node):
    if isinstance(node, (ast.For, ast.AsyncFor, ast.NamedExpr)):
      return stored([node.target])
    if isinstance(node, (ast.With, ast.AsyncWith)):
      return stored(item.optional_vars for item in node.items
                    if item.optional_vars is not None)
    if isinstance(node, (ast.ExceptHandler, ast.MatchAs, ast.MatchStar)):
      return [node.name] if node.name else []
    if isinstance(node, ast.MatchMapping):
      return [node.rest] if node.rest else []
    if isinstance(node, (ast.Import, ast.ImportFrom)):
      return [alias.asname or alias.name.split(".")[0]
              for alias in node.names]
    return []

  found = []
  for function in ast.walk(ast.parse(source)):
    if not isinstance(function, (ast.FunctionDef, ast.AsyncFunctionDef)):
      continue
    arguments = function.args
    parameters = {
      a.arg for a in (arguments.posonlyargs + arguments.args
                      + arguments.kwonlyargs
                      + [arguments.vararg, arguments.kwarg])
      if a is not None
    } - {"self", "cls"}
    for node in own_nodes(function):
      found += [f"{function.name}:{node.lineno} rebinds {name}"
                for name in bindings(node) if name in parameters]
  return found

