\paragraph{The batch as a whole.}
\label{sec:decide-batch-check}
A reasons file can pass every line check and the quote check and still
not be a screener's work: a screener given several hundred records
that read some and filled the rest with one reason and one confidence
writes well-formed lines (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/291}).
So once the ids are resolved, after the quote check and before
anything is saved, the batch is checked as a whole with the review
module's [[batch_findings]] (\cref{sec:batch-check} there, which states
the thresholds and why they are what they are): a reason shared by at
least ten records with different titles, each reported apart, and one
confidence on every row of a file of twenty rows or more.  The file is
where the confidence can be judged, since it may be one screener's
batch; a stored run cannot (\cref{sec:stored-batches} there).
[[sessions screen --verdicts-file]]
(\cref{sec:sessions-screen}) runs the same chunk.

The rows are the file's lines in the file's order, one per line, each
with the title of the record its id names; a line's confidence is its
own column, never [[-c]], which is the same for every line by
construction.  A single [[--reason]] for many records shares its
reason by construction too: it is how a rule is applied on purpose,
so it is checked as one, and reported as information (\enquote{Noted:
one reason for 50 records\dots}), which nothing refuses.  A command
without a reason has nothing to check.

The findings go to standard error, each with its count and the first
[[BATCH_PAPERS_SHOWN]] ids, the rest counted, so that a screener can
look at the rows the check means.  By default the batch is then saved,
as with the quote check: the check is a heuristic, a batch it flags may
be honest, and scripts written before it must keep working.
[[--strict]] refuses the batch instead, unsaved and with status~2, on
any finding that is not information \emph{and} on any quote not found,
so that one option stops a run on everything the checks of a batch can
see.  [[--strict-quotes]] stays as it was, the narrower option, and
refuses on quotes alone, before the batch check is printed.  Under
[[--strict]] every finding is printed before the refusal, so a
screener sees all that is wrong in one run.

<<sessions command>>=
BATCH_PAPERS_SHOWN = 5


def batch_rows_of(lines, by_id: dict, by_doi: dict) -> list:
    """The batch check's rows of a reasons or verdicts file, in its order.

    Each row has the title of the first decision its id names; a
    verdicts file's lines also give the verdict and tags.
    """
    from scholar.review import BatchRow

    rows = []
    for entry in sorted(lines, key=lambda entry: entry.line):
        matches = decisions_named_by(entry.paper, by_id, by_doi)
        rows.append(BatchRow(
            paper=entry.paper,
            title=matches[0].paper.title if matches else None,
            reason=entry.reason,
            confidence=entry.confidence,
            status=getattr(entry, "status", None),
            tags=tuple(getattr(entry, "tags", ())),
            batch=entry.batch,
        ))
    return rows


def one_reason_rows(decisions, reason: str) -> list:
    """The batch check's rows of one `--reason`, one per paper."""
    from scholar.review import BatchRow

    papers = {d.paper.id: d.paper for d in decisions}
    return [BatchRow(paper=paper_id, title=paper.title, reason=reason)
            for paper_id, paper in papers.items()]


def batch_finding_lines(findings) -> list[str]:
    """One line per finding: its detail and the first ids it concerns."""
    lines = []
    for finding in findings:
        shown = ", ".join(finding.papers[:BATCH_PAPERS_SHOWN])
        more = len(finding.papers) - BATCH_PAPERS_SHOWN
        line = f"{finding.detail}: {shown}" + (
            f" \N{HORIZONTAL ELLIPSIS}and {more} more" if more > 0 else ""
        )
        lines.append(f"Noted: {line}" if finding.information else line)
    return lines
