A file that names its batches ([[# batch:]] lines,
\cref{sec:batch-ids-in-files}) is also checked batch by batch, since a
batch is one screener's piece of work: the file as a whole may be the
concatenation of several screeners' batches, of which one was
defaulted, and that one is then a small share of the file.  The
confidence in particular is judged per batch, where a defaulted batch
shows one value on every row although the file as a whole does not.
Each batch's findings are listed under its id after the file's; rows
before the first [[# batch:]] line are in no batch.  A batch that holds
every row of the file (a file of one batch, or [[--batch]]) is the file
again and is not checked twice.  The thresholds are the same; under
[[--strict]] a finding in any batch refuses the run.

A reason shared in a batch is also shared in the file, so it would be
reported twice and counted twice under [[--strict]].  It is reported
once ([[without_repeated_reasons]]): under its batch when the file's
finding concerns the same records (the batch's count equals the
file's), else in the file's line, which then says how many of its
records are in each batch that shares it, and the batch's line is left
out.

