\paragraph{Checking the reasons a session stores.}
\label{sec:sessions-check-batches}
The batch check runs when a batch is recorded, so reasons written
before it existed went in unchecked.  [[scholar sessions check-batches
S]] examines them with [[stored_batch_check]] (\cref{sec:stored-batches}
in the review module).  It reports first the reasons shared by at least
ten records with different titles across the whole session, the agent
reasons and the screenings' reasons apart, since a screener's batch is
spread over several stored runs and several batches are merged into
one; then, per stored run, the runs whose rows all carry one reason,
as a note; and last, in words, what it cannot judge from stored runs:
one confidence on every row, which needs a batch id stored with each
row (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/292}).  The output
says what a stored run is, so that nobody reads a run as a screener's
batch.  A finding is worded as what it is, \enquote{shared by N records
with different titles}, for a reader to judge: some such reasons are
honest (correction notices of one kind), others are defaulted rows.

It is a command of its own rather than a flag of [[sessions show]],
which prints a session, or of [[sessions check]], which checks a
session against a semantic search: this one reads only the stored
reasons, and its output is a list of findings.  It is read-only, never
saves, and exits~0 whatever it finds, since it reports on decisions
already made; [[-f json]] gives the shared reasons and every run, for a
script.  The help states the thresholds, which are scholar's own
choice, explained in the review module; [[sessions decide]] and
[[sessions screen]] point to it.

<<sessions command>>=
@sessions_app.command("check-batches")
def sessions_check_batches(
    name: Annotated[
        str,
        typer.Argument(
            help="Session name or query whose stored reasons to check.",
            autocompletion=complete_session_name,
        ),
    ],
    output_format: Annotated[
        str,
        typer.Option(
            "--format", "-f",
            help="Output format: table or json.",
            autocompletion=complete_display_format,
        ),
    ] = "table",
) -> None:
    """
    Check the stored agent reasons and screenings of a session.

    Lists each reason shared by at least 10 records with different
    titles, across the whole session (agent reasons and screening
    reasons apart), for a reader to judge: correction notices of one
    kind may honestly share one, defaulted rows do too. Then lists the
    stored runs (one `sessions decide` or `sessions screen` command
    each) whose rows all carry one reason: a --reason, a rule applied
    on purpose, or the same reason on every line. A stored run is not
    a screener's batch: a batch is split over runs by verdict and tags,
    and several batches can be merged into one run, so one confidence
    on every row is not judged from runs. Rows recorded with a batch id
    (a `# batch:` line or --batch) are also checked per stored batch,
    one screener's rows under one id: shared reasons and one confidence
    on every row. Read-only; exits 0.

    The thresholds are scholar's own choice. A reason shared by at
    least 10 records with different titles is reported (in nine
    sessions measured on 2026-11-20 honest reasons were shared by at
    most 7, defaulted ones by 13 and more). When a file is given to
    `sessions decide` or `sessions screen`, one confidence on every row
    is reported from 20 rows.
    """
    from scholar.review import stored_batch_check

    if output_format not in ("table", "json"):
        Console().print(
            f"[red]Unknown format: {escape(output_format)}; use table or "
            "json.[/red]"
        )
        raise typer.Exit(2)
    session = open_session_argument(name)
    if session is None:
        Console().print(f"[red]Session not found: {escape(name)}[/red]")
        raise typer.Exit(1)
    check = stored_batch_check(session)
    if output_format == "json":
        print(json.dumps(check_batches_data(check), indent=2))
        return
    for line in check_batches_lines(check, name):
        Console().print(escape(line), soft_wrap=True, highlight=False)
