@

The refusal of a human's decision lists the decisions it found, and
says how to leave them out and where a human redoes their own.

[[--batch ID]] selects by the batch an agent's reasons came from
([[agent_batch]], \cref{sec:batch-ids-in-files}), so that a batch the
batch check flags (\cref{sec:sessions-check-batches}) can be redone as
a unit: reset, then screened again.  Like [[--source]] it selects on
its own and narrows the other selectors, and it is repeatable.  The
value is read as the file's id is read ([[read_batch_id]]), so the
white space of a copied id does not matter.  It selects decisions only.
A screening recorded from the batch is another screener's verdict
beside the decision and is not reset by this; a reset of the paper sets
its screenings aside as any reset does (\cref{sec:reset-decision} in
the review module).  A human's decision or confirmation in the batch
refuses the run as below, so a caller resets the agent's part with
[[--source agent]].  A batch id no decision carries is noted, not
refused: a reset clears the id with the rest of the trace (it is kept
in the reset history), so a second run finds nothing to do, as a
second [[--source llm]] does.

<<read the batches to reset>>=
wanted_batches: list[str] = []
for value in batches or []:
    wanted, problem = read_batch_id(value)
    if problem:
        console.print(f"[red]--batch: {escape(problem)}[/red]")
        raise typer.Exit(2)
    wanted_batches.append(wanted)
<<note the batches no decision carries>>=
held_batches = {d.agent_batch for d in session.decisions}
for wanted in wanted_batches:
    if wanted not in held_batches:
        Console(stderr=True).print(
            f"[yellow]No decision carries batch {escape(wanted)} (a reset "
            "clears it, so a batch already reset is not found "
            "again).[/yellow]",
            soft_wrap=True,
        )
