\subsection{CSV formatter}

For scripting and shell pipelines, we provide a tab-separated CSV format.
This is especially useful when output is piped to other commands.
We include metadata as a comment header so consumers know the search context.
The [[# Results:]] line is the count a script records per query and
provider, so it is where a failure must show: it reads
[[failed (reason)]] instead of 0 (or [[N, incomplete (reason)]]),
deliberately not a number, so a script that parses it stops instead of
recording a zero.  Retries get a line of their own, which keeps the
count of an answered search a plain number, and so do the total the
provider reported ([[# Total reported:]]) and a count that stopped at
the limit ([[# Capped at limit:]]), for the same reason
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/166}).  A merged result
adds one [[# Search:]] line per provider with that provider's own
outcome (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/164}):

<<formatter classes>>=
class CSVFormatter:
    """Output results as tab-separated values for scripting."""

    def __init__(self, spreadsheet_safe: bool = False) -> None:
        self.spreadsheet_safe = spreadsheet_safe

    def format(self, results: list) -> None:
        """Output results as CSV with metadata header."""
        <<choose how the CSV formatter writes a text cell>>
        for result in results:
            # Metadata header as comments
            print(csv_comment(f"Query: {result.query}"))
            print(csv_comment(f"Provider: {result.provider}"))
            print(csv_comment(f"Timestamp: {result.timestamp}"))
            label = hits_label(
                len(result.papers), result.error,
                ranked=is_ranked_provider(result.provider),
            )
            print(csv_comment(f"Results: {label}"))
            if result.retries:
                print(csv_comment(f"Retries: {result.retries}"))
            total, estimated = result_total(result)
            if total is not None:
                about = " (estimate)" if estimated else ""
                print(csv_comment(f"Total reported: {total}{about}"))
            if (not result.error and result.limit
                    and not getattr(result, "parts", None)
                    and len(result.papers) == result.limit
                    and (total is None or total > len(result.papers))):
                print(csv_comment(f"Capped at limit: {result.limit}"))
            for line in part_hits_lines(result):
                print(csv_comment(f"Search: {line}"))
            print()
            <<write the header and the papers as tab-separated rows>>
            print()
@

\paragraph{The rows.}
\label{sec:search-csv-rows}
The header and the papers are rows, and [[csv.writer]] writes them;
the [[#]] lines and the blank lines around the rows are not rows and
are printed as before.  The rows used to be printed with an f-string,
fields joined by tabs and nothing quoted, so a tab or a line break
inside a title, an author name or a venue started a new cell or a new
row, and no reader could tell
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/300}).  Metadata
cleaning at ingest does not prevent it: [[clean_metadata_text]] keeps
tabs and line breaks, and it even makes them from the entities
[[&#9;]] and [[&#10;]] (tried), and cached and saved papers can predate
any cleaning anyway.  The standard library's [[csv]] module is the
tool for delimited text, and no dependency of the project does the
job better.  Its documentation
(\url{https://docs.python.org/3/library/csv.html}, read 2026-11-20)
says that the default quoting, [[QUOTE_MINIMAL]], quotes only
\enquote{fields which contain special characters such as delimiter,
quotechar, '\textbackslash r', '\textbackslash n' or any of the
characters in lineterminator}, doubling a quote mark inside, and that
the reader \enquote{is hard-coded to recognise either '\textbackslash
r' or '\textbackslash n' as end-of-line}.  So a reader with the same
settings gets every field back whatever it holds (the round trip in
the tests), and a row whose fields hold no tab, line break or quote
mark is written exactly as the f-string wrote it (tested byte for
byte).  The tab stays the delimiter, since the scripts that read this
output split on it, and the comma would change every row.  The line
terminator is set to a line feed, since the module's default is a
carriage return and a line feed, which would change every row as well.
Quoting is the change: a field holding a quote mark, a tab or a line
break is now quoted, with its quote marks doubled.  Titles with quote
marks are the case that will meet it most; in the invented sample of
the tests, three titles of twenty hold one, and only those rows
change.  The rows go to [[sys.stdout]] as it is when [[format]] runs,
where [[print]] writes the [[#]] lines, so the two stay in order.  The
tests are in \cref{sec:search-csv-rows-tests}, next to those of
[[--spreadsheet-safe]], since the formatter's own tests are methods of
one shared class.

\paragraph{The comment lines.}
\label{sec:search-csv-comments}
The output is a sequence of blocks, one per result: [[#]] lines, a
blank line, the header, the paper rows, a blank line.  A reader parses
the whole output with [[csv.reader(f, delimiter="\t")]], takes the
rows up to the first empty row as the block's comments, the next row
as the header and the rows up to the next empty row as papers.  It
must not drop the lines that start with [[#]] before parsing: a quoted
field can hold a line break followed by [[#]], and a title can begin
with [[#]].  The empty rows are reliable separators, since a comment
row starts with [[#]] and a paper row has six fields, empty or not.

That recipe needs every comment to be one row of one field, and the
[[#]] lines print text from outside: the query as typed, provider
names, error reasons from the services.  A tab in such a line split it
into fields, and a tab or line break followed by a quote mark made the
reader open a quoted field that swallowed the header and every row
(the query [[x]], tab, [["y]] did, tried).  So [[csv_comment]] turns
tab, carriage return and line feed in a comment into spaces.  Quote
marks are left as they are: the reader opens a quoted field only where
a field begins with one, and a comment row is a single field beginning
with [[#]], so its quote marks are read as text (a query in quote marks
stays its own comment row in the tests).  A comment of ordinary text is
printed as before.

<<formatter classes>>=
CSV_COMMENT_BREAKS = re.compile("[\t\r\n]")


def csv_comment(text: str) -> str:
    """Return `text` as one `#` line of `search -f csv` output.

    Tab, carriage return and line feed become spaces, so the line stays
    one row of one field for `csv.reader(f, delimiter="\\t")`.
    """
    return "# " + CSV_COMMENT_BREAKS.sub(" ", text)
<<write the header and the papers as tab-separated rows>>=
rows = csv.writer(sys.stdout, delimiter="\t", lineterminator="\n")
rows.writerow(["title", "authors", "year", "doi", "venue", "url"])
for paper in result.papers:
    rows.writerow([
        cell(paper.title),
        cell("; ".join(paper.authors)),
        number(str(paper.year) if paper.year else ""),
        cell(paper.doi or ""),
        cell(paper.venue or ""),
        cell(paper.url or ""),
    ])
@

\paragraph{Spreadsheet-safe cells.}
\label{sec:search-csv-spreadsheet-safe}
[[search -f csv --spreadsheet-safe]] writes the text cells of the data
rows (title, authors, DOI, venue, URL) through
[[spreadsheet_safe_cell]], the rule of the session export
(\cref{sec:spreadsheet-safe} in the review module, where the reference
and its limits are given): a cell that begins with a character a
spreadsheet program may read as the start of a formula, or with the
quote itself, gets one [[']] in front.  The year goes through
[[spreadsheet_safe_number]]: a number stays as it is, a year stored as
text is treated like a text cell.  The prefix is put on the cell and
then [[csv.writer]] writes it (\cref{sec:search-csv-rows}), so a cell
that begins with a tab or a line break is quoted with the prefix inside
and stays one cell: the text after the tab never begins a cell of its
own.  When the rows were printed unquoted, a title that begins with a
tab and then [[=1+2]] came out as [[']], a tab and [[=1+2]], and
LibreOffice Calc made a formula of the second cell; the quoted row it
reads as one text cell (both tried on 2026-11-20, tab as separator).
The [[#]] lines above the header begin with [[#]], which is not such a
character, so they are left alone, and so is the header, whose names
are fixed.  Without the option every cell is written as stored, which
is what a script reads.

<<choose how the CSV formatter writes a text cell>>=
if self.spreadsheet_safe:
    from scholar.review import spreadsheet_safe_cell as cell
    from scholar.review import spreadsheet_safe_number as number
else:
    def cell(value):
        return value
    number = cell
@

The option is the same on [[search]] and on [[sessions export]], and so
is its help, which says exactly what it changes in a cell and how to
undo it, because a script that reads such a file must know.  It is off
by default: the issue
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/285}) left open whether
the guard should be on by default, an option, or only documented, and
an option is the choice that changes nothing for the scripts that read
the CSV today.  Turning it on by default takes one line,
[[SPREADSHEET_SAFE_DEFAULT = True]]; the flag has a negative form,
[[--no-spreadsheet-safe]], so that the raw file can still be asked for
then.  The option's value is [[None]] when neither form is given, which
lets the commands tell an explicit flag from the default.

<<constants>>=
