\section{Entry Point}
\label{sec:cli-main}

The main entry point runs the Typer application.
It registers nothing itself: the snowball and tuxedo subcommands are part of
the application's group class and are imported only when they run
(\cref{sec:lazy-subcommands}).

Many commands read or write notes and per-query decisions, from the
[[notes]] commands to the TUI.  When that file is unreadable, or a write
fails, the notes module raises [[NotesFileError]] instead of carrying on
with an empty store and overwriting the file
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/114}).  Rather than catch it
in every command, we catch it once here and turn it into one line that
names the file and points to its [[.bak]], with exit code~1.

A save of a session that cannot take the session file's lock in time
raises [[SessionNotSaved]] (\cref{sec:session-lock} in the review
chapter), from whichever command saved: a decision, an append, the
TUI on exit.  It is caught here too, and printed as the one line it
is, unwrapped, so that the command it ends with can be copied whole,
and without a traceback; the exit code is~1, or 130 when the user
stopped the wait with Ctrl-C, the status of an interrupted command.
The review module is imported only when an error arrives, since
importing it for every command would slow the commands that never touch
a session.

What the refused command leaves behind is its session in the kept file,
and whatever it stored elsewhere before it saved.  Two commands print
nothing of their work, since they save before they show it.
[[search -n]] persists the session before it displays the results
(\enquote{we persist before displaying}, above), so a refused save
prints no results: the
search's records and papers are in the kept file, and the providers'
answers in their caches, so a run after the recovery is answered from
the cache.  [[llm synthesize]] saves the session, with the abstracts
its enrichment found, before it writes the synthesis, so a refused save
writes and prints no synthesis: the enrichment is in the kept file,
and the prose the model wrote is in the synthesis cache, which
[[cache clear]] keeps (\cref{cache-module}).

<<main entry point>>=
def main() -> None:
    """Main entry point for the Scholar CLI."""
    from scholar.notes import NotesFileError
    from scholar.utils import backup_path

    try:
        app()
    except NotesFileError as error:
        Console(stderr=True).print(
            f"[red]{escape(str(error))}[/red]\n"
            "Nothing was overwritten. Repair the file or restore its "
            f"previous version from {escape(str(backup_path(error.path)))}."
        )
        sys.exit(1)
    except Exception as error:
        from scholar.review import SessionNotSaved

        if not isinstance(error, SessionNotSaved):
            raise
        print_session_not_saved(error)
        sys.exit(130 if error.interrupted else 1)


def print_session_not_saved(error: Exception) -> None:
    """Print a refused save's error on stderr as one unwrapped line."""
    Console(stderr=True).print(
        f"[red]{escape(str(error))}[/red]", soft_wrap=True
    )
@

A corrupt notes file therefore ends [[scholar notes list]] with that line
and exit code~1, and the file is left as it was:

<<test functions>>=
def test_main_reports_unreadable_notes_file(tmp_path, monkeypatch, capsys):
    """main() turns NotesFileError into one line and exit code 1."""
    from scholar.notes import NOTES_FILE, get_data_dir, invalidate_notes_cache

    monkeypatch.setenv("SCHOLAR_DATA_DIR", str(tmp_path))
    invalidate_notes_cache()
    notes_file = get_data_dir() / NOTES_FILE
    notes_file.write_text('{"doi:10.1/x": ')
    monkeypatch.setattr(sys, "argv", ["scholar", "notes", "list"])
    with pytest.raises(SystemExit) as exit_info:
        main()
    assert exit_info.value.code == 1
    err = capsys.readouterr().err
    assert "Cannot read notes file" in " ".join(err.split())
    assert "paper_notes.json.bak" in "".join(err.split())
    assert notes_file.read_text() == '{"doi:10.1/x": '
